"A bird does not have to flap all the time. With its wings held out, it can glide, slowly losing height as it goes. And if it finds a place where warm air is rising, it can circle inside it and be carried upward for nothing. Eagles and hawks travel great distances this way.": "156b6e27187b",
"A bird landed on the fence.": "8d3452df3cfb",
"A feather is light but strong.": "241fc0ca2d07",
"Air moving over a wing gives lift.": "7999913e0202",
"Feathers matter just as much. Each one is light and stiff. Its tiny branches hook on to one another like a zipper, so the wing makes a smooth surface that air cannot slip through. A bird keeps its feathers in order by running them through its beak.": "1fb92d0c4dcc",
"Flapping is hard work, and a bird's body is built for it. Many of its bones are hollow, with thin struts inside to keep them strong. Its breastbone has a deep ridge called a keel, and the flight muscles are fixed to it. In many birds those muscles make up a quarter or more of the whole body.": "b6cfdc0c5b1d",
"Flying takes strong muscles.": "0bb23baa9d9e",
"Hold your hand flat in the wind from a fan, with the front edge tipped up a little. You will feel the air push your hand upward. A bird's wing does the same thing, but it does it much better. The upward push is called lift.": "f2a27897577e",
"Hold your hand flat in the wind from a fan, with the front edge tipped up a little. You will feel the air push your hand upward. A bird's wing does the same thing, but it does it much better. The upward push is called lift. Lift only comes when air is flowing over the wing, so a bird has to keep moving forward. That is what flapping is for. When the wings beat down, they push air down and back, and the bird is pushed up and forward. On the way up, the wings fold in a little, so that they do not push the bird back down. Flapping is hard work, and a bird's body is built for it. Many of its bones are hollow, with thin struts inside to keep them strong. Its breastbone has a deep ridge called a keel, and the flight muscles are fixed to it. In many birds those muscles make up a quarter or more of the whole body. Feathers matter just as much. Each one is light and stiff. Its tiny branches hook on to one another like a zipper, so the wing makes a smooth surface that air cannot slip through. A bird keeps its feathers in order by running them through its beak. A bird does not have to flap all the time. With its wings held out, it can glide, slowly losing height as it goes. And if it finds a place where warm air is rising, it can circle inside it and be carried upward for nothing. Eagles and hawks travel great distances this way. Wings come in different shapes for different lives. Short, rounded wings are good for quick turns among trees. Long, narrow wings are good for gliding over the sea for hours. You can often tell how a bird lives just by looking at the shape of its wings.": "09f042a69b0d",
